export function sparseRewardPreservation({p, eq, table, cite, related}){return {
opening:p("Dense token prediction provides a target at every observed position. A terminal verifier may return one scalar after thousands of autoregressive steps. Counting external labels per generated token exposes a real bandwidth and generation-cost difference. It does not measure usefulness for a specific downstream task.")+eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}L_{\mathrm{NTP}}&=\mathbb E_{x\sim\mathcal D}\!\left[-\sum_t\log\pi_\theta(x_t\mid x_{<t})\right]\\J_{\mathrm{RL}}&=\mathbb E_{x\sim\mathcal P, \, \tau\sim\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid x)}[R(x, \tau)]\end{aligned}`, "D is a text distribution; P is a prompt distribution;τ is a trajectory. These are different objectives, not comparable values on one numerical scale.")+table("Three questions that reward bandwidth alone cannot answer", ["Quantity", "Question", "Sparse outcome RL"], [["External supervision bandwidth", "How much information does the evaluator return?", "One terminal scalar; binary entropy depends on success rate"], ["Objective alignment", "Does the update target the evaluated outcome?", "Potentially direct, but only as good as the verifier"], ["Estimator quality", "How noisy, biased or temporally ambiguous is the update?", "Can have high variance and poor token-level credit"]]),
credit:p("The reward multiplies a structured score gradient. Sampled actions, prefixes, policy probabilities, model derivatives and the baseline supply its direction. One bit can select a high-dimensional update without encoding an arbitrary missing strategy. A successful trace can reinforce decisive steps and incidental wording together; failed traces may contain useful partial work.")+eq(String.raw`\langle g_{\mathrm{train}}, g_{\mathrm{target}}\rangle`, "This inner product is an informal local alignment diagnostic, not the variance-based SNR above. Its magnitude also depends on gradient scale.")+p("An update can align with a narrow verifier and remain a noisy estimate. Reducing learning rate changes parameter displacement, not the information in one sample. Batching independent estimates reduces variance; momentum can aggregate estimates over time. None of these proves that most pretraining gradients are statistically noise."),
support:p("Here p is per-attempt success probability. Practical support means useful behavior is reachable within the rollout budget, not merely assigned positive softmax probability.")+table("Synthetic independent attempts:p=0.001 per rollout", ["Attempts K", "Chance of at least one success", "Kp approximation"], [["1", "0.10%", "0.10%"], ["100", "9.52%", "10%"], ["1, 000", "63.23%", "100%:no longer a useful approximation"]])+p("For rare successes, 1−(1−p)^K≈Kp requires Kp to remain small. At a larger budget, the probability saturates below one rather than growing linearly forever. Dense training, tools, a changed task mixture or better sampling may be needed before policy-gradient updates can usefully select successes.")+p("Pretraining supplies language, knowledge, representations and action primitives. Targeted SFT or mid-training can raise initial success and reduce the policy shift needed under limited updates, KL/clipping and rollout budgets. Exploration still searches decompositions, code edits, tool calls and recovery strategies. New combinations need not have appeared verbatim in demonstrations.")+p("The prompt distribution, task generator, compiler, tests, tools, environment dynamics and curriculum contain reusable information too. Count these external artifacts when comparing information budgets. A rich verifier can still expose only pass/fail for one attempted trace.")+p(related("math/information", "Entropy and information accounting")),
rejection:eq(String.raw`L_{\mathrm{RSFT}}=-\mathbb E_{\tau\sim\pi_{\mathrm{old}}(\cdot\mid R=1)}\!\left[\sum_t\log\pi_\theta(a_t\mid s_t)\right]`, "Conditioning on success requires nonzero success probability. This supervised update holds the selected sampling distribution fixed.")+table("Compare feedback transformations at matched rollout and compute budgets", ["Method", "Feedback on sampled history", "Tradeoff"], [["Successful-trace SFT", "Positive token targets from retained successes", "Discarded failures and narrowed modes; refresh frequency matters"], ["Policy-gradient RL", "Signed advantage on sampled score gradients", "Fresh candidates and negative evidence, but noisy credit"], ["On-policy distillation", "Teacher token feedback at student-generated prefixes", "Dense and locally relevant, but teacher preferences can include incidental style"]])+p("A successful policy can be distilled rather than rediscovered by search. Dense teacher feedback is valuable when it targets useful behavior; more token feedback does not by itself prove better alignment with external correctness. Compare diversity, filtering, sequence length, feedback quality and refresh cadence, not only label count."),
evidence:p("The bandwidth/alignment/prior explanation is a framework, not a proven general theory. It suggests rapid narrow gains when probability allocation changes, uneven domain improvements, cold-start failures and sensitivity to an optimized reward proxy. These predictions do not identify the cause of every observed gain.")+table("Claims that still need separate evidence", ["Hypothesis", "What must not be conflated"], [["Most pretraining gradients are noise for downstream reward", "Objective mismatch versus estimator variance"], ["Annealing and RL gains share one landscape mechanism", "Learning-rate changes versus targeted reward optimization"], ["LLM on-policy sensitivity comes from being far down a scaling curve", "Importance-ratio accumulation versus a landscape metaphor"], ["Iterative self-SFT is generally inferior to RL", "Refresh, selection, diversity and compute controls"], ["Classical RL scaling limits primarily follow reward bit rate", "Representation learning, exploration and system costs"], ["Large-scale learning should always favor low bias/high variance", "Task-specific variance, bias and finite-budget tradeoffs"]])+p("Classical RL contains on-policy and off-policy families. Replay is not universally safe there or universally invalid for LLMs. Long-trajectory ratio products already explain one concrete mismatch risk; clipping or truncation changes the correction. A pretrained-policy refinement problem differs from jointly learning representations and actions from a weak prior.")+p("Before scaling, measure per-prompt pass@k, reward entropy, group contrast and held-out general behavior. Audit reward integrity and sampling provenance. Compare RL, rejection SFT and teacher feedback with matched rollout tokens and accelerator time. Dense process feedback is useful only when its added credit outweighs evaluator bias and exploitability."),
sources:p(cite("https://www.beren.io/2026-07-26-How-Can-LLM-RL-Work-Despite-Information-Theoretic-Inefficiency/", "Millidge:the motivating framework")+" · "+cite("https://www.tobyord.com/writing/inefficiency-of-reinforcement-learning", "Ord:RL inefficiency")+" · "+cite("https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/bits-per-sample", "Patel:bits per sample")+" · "+cite("https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00992696", "Williams:REINFORCE")+" · "+cite("https://arxiv.org/abs/1011.0686", "Ross et al.:imitation and structured prediction"))
};}
